%% TEST-NAME: test-concept-in-restatable-intro-teaser
%% TEST-WHAT: Verify the user's core scenario: a \restate{thm:main}
%%            teaser in the introduction containing \Hom*, with
%%            the actual \restatable{theorem}{thm:main}{body with
%%            \Hom*} later in the main body. The def site MUST be
%%            the main-body atom (the original declaration firing,
%%            where the counter alias is intact), NEVER the intro
%%            teaser atom.
%% TEST-SOURCE: DESIGN.md §8a.5.a save/clear/restore, §8a.9
%%              Interaction with restatable
%% TEST-SECTION: DESIGN.md §8a.5.a, §8a.9
%% TEST-EXIT: 0
%% TEST-LOG-NOT: semtex.*Error|Undefined control sequence
%% TEST-LOG-NOT: Package semtex Warning
%% TEST-SBL-CONTAINS: \semtex@sbl@end{OK}
%% TEST-SBL-COUNT: \semtex@sbl@def{1.1}{Hom} = 0
%% TEST-SBL-COUNT: \semtex@sbl@def{2.1}{Hom} = 1
%% TEST-AUX-CONTAINS: \semtex@concept{Hom}{2.1}
%% TEST-AUX-NOT-CONTAINS: \semtex@concept{Hom}{1.1}
%% TEST-PACKAGES: thmtools,hyperref,cleveref,amsthm,semtex
%% TEST-RERUN: 2
%%
%% The intro section 1 contains a \restate{thm:main} teaser. Inside
%% the restated body, \Hom* fires. Without the §8a.5.a save/clear/
%% restore extension, \semtex@currentatom would still hold the
%% intro paragraph's atom number (1.1) and \Hom* would erroneously
%% register 1.1 as the def site. With the extension, the restate
%% branch clears currentatom to \@empty, so \Hom* sees empty and
%% silently no-ops.
%%
%% Later, in section 2, the \restatable{theorem}{thm:main}{...}
%% fires for the first time. The counter alias \c@theorem = \c@atom
%% is intact, the normal hook body runs, \semtex@currentatom is
%% set to the theorem atom's number (2.1), and the \Hom* inside
%% the body registers 2.1 as the def site.
%%
%% Assertions verify that ONLY 2.1 is recorded as the def site,
%% never 1.1. Atom numbering assumption: section 1 intro paragraph
%% = 1.1 (first atom); restate{thm:main} teaser no-ops as a
%% sub-atom. Section 2 restatable theorem = 2.1 (first atom in
%% section 2). If the numbering assumption proves off-by-one
%% against the actual §8a.5 clearing semantics, the fixture may
%% need adjustment once implementation lands.
%%
%% Until §8a.5.a extension + §8a.9 are implemented, this fixture
%% FAILS: the \Hom* inside the restate teaser would register 1.1
%% against currentatom, producing the wrong def site.

\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{thmtools}
\usepackage{hyperref}
\usepackage{cleveref}
\usepackage{amsthm}
\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}[section]
\usepackage{semtex}
\semtextrack{theorem}

\semtexNewCommand{\Hom}[2]{\mathrm{Hom}(#1,#2)}

\begin{document}
\START

\section{Introduction}

We tease a result early. The teaser below contains \verb|\Hom*|
inside the restated body, but the save/clear/restore of
currentatom in §8a.5.a ensures the star does NOT register
against this intro paragraph's atom.

\restate{thmMain}

The paragraph after the teaser resumes normal atom flow.

\section{Main}

\begin{restatable}{theorem}{thmMain}\label{thm:main}
  The $\Hom*{X}{Y}$ set has the expected universal property.
  This is the ORIGINAL declaration firing, with the counter
  alias intact, so the \verb|\Hom*| here is the one that
  registers the def site.
\end{restatable}

Later uses of $\Hom{A}{B}$ resolve via the concept map to the
main-body atom, not the intro teaser.

\END
\end{document}
